{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>According to their journeys, by the mouth of YHWH.</b> He explains that the journeys were by the mouth of YHWH. It also includes that Moshe wrote all the journeys, every one, by the mouth of YHWH, and the will of the Blessed Name in this account was for the benefit I have mentioned, and yet another benefit: to recall the lovingkindnesses of HaShem. For even though He decreed to toss them about and move them in the wilderness, do not suppose that they were moving and being tossed from journey to journey the entire forty years with no rest — for in all forty years in the wilderness they had only forty-two journeys, all of which took place between the first year and the last year, which is the fortieth year. For in the first year they made fourteen journeys from Ra'mses to Ritmah, from where the spies were dispatched, as it says (Numbers 12:16) ״ואחר נסעו העם חצרות״, and it is written ״ויסעו מחצרות ויחנו ברתמה״ — and that is the wilderness of Paran, from where the spies were dispatched; and from there it was said to Moshe, שלח לך. And it is called Ritmah on account of the evil tongue of the spies, as it says (Psalms 120:3–4): ״מה יתן לך ומה יוסיף לך לשון רמיה / חצי גבור שנונים עם גחלי רתמים״. And eight journeys they made after the death of Aharon, from Mount Hor to the plains of Moav, in the fortieth year — that makes twenty-two journeys. It follows that for all thirty-eight years they traveled only twenty journeys, and of these thirty-eight years, for half the time they had rest in Kadesh, as it is written (Deuteronomy 1:46): ״ותשבו בקדש ימים רבים״, and they said in Seder Olam: nineteen years they stayed in Kadesh — and that is ״ימים רבים כימים אשר ישבתם,״ corresponding to the other nineteen years among the remaining journeys. Thus you learn that all their wandering in the wilderness lasted only nineteen years, during which they made nineteen journeys." "<b>And behold,</b> their travel was not orderly — in one place they would stay nineteen years, and in another place only a day or a night. But everything was by divine measure, and according to the lifting of the cloud. And in order to make known that they were not walking in the wilderness as wanderers and the lost, but were delighting in it and had their full provision, more than those who dwell in settled land — therefore the Rabbis, of blessed memory, likened the Torah's account of these journeys to a parable. To what may the matter be compared? To a king whose son was ill, and he took him to a distant place to heal him there. When they were returning, the father began counting all the journeys and saying to him: here we sat, here we were frightened, here your head ached." "<b>And furthermore,</b> there is yet another benefit in this, to make known to us that everything follows according to the intention. And for this reason the journeys were enumerated by their names, to indicate that everything depends on the disposition of Israel's thoughts toward the Holy One, blessed be He. When they were worthy and did the will of HaShem, and they traveled in their thoughts on those journeys from a bad disposition to a good one, the attribute of mercy that accompanied them would have compassion and shield them — and therefore ״ויחנו בהר שפר״ and ״ויחנו במתקה״. And when they sinned and traveled from a good disposition to a bad one, the attribute of judgment that was with them was drawn taut against them to punish them — and therefore ״ויחנו בחרדה״ and ״ויחנו בדפקה״ and ״ויחנו במרה״. And on this they said in the Yerushalmi: when Israel would complain during the journeys, the watchmen would circle the camp from fright at nights. They explained to us thereby that when they complained during the journeys and shifted their disposition and altered it, the attributes that accompanied them day and night would act upon them, whether for good or for ill, all according to their disposition. And that is what is said (Song of Songs 3:8): ״מפחד בלילות״ — that is, that Israel would be frightened by the attribute of judgment." "<b>And furthermore,</b> the account of the journeys includes another benefit, for it alludes to the future. For all the words of the prophets demonstrate that the final redemption is like the first one; and just as Israel went out in the first redemption from Egypt to the wilderness, so in the final redemption many of Israel are destined to go out to the wilderness and pass through these places, and the Holy One, blessed be He, will sustain them and lead them there as He did for Israel in the wilderness. And that is what the prophet said (Ezekiel 20:35): ״והוצאתים אל מדבר העמים״ — and this verse speaks of the future, in the final redemption. And the verse alludes to this from the fact that it mentions here twice the expression מוצאיהם: it writes ״ויכתוב משה את מוצאיהם״ and then repeats, ״ואלה מסעיהם למוצאיהם״ — for the first is Israel's departure from Egypt, and the second is our departure from this exile. And since he had already begun to mention אלה מסעי concerning the journeys of the past, therefore he said ״ואלה מסעיהם״ concerning the future, in the second departure, in the manner of what is written (Isaiah 11:11): ״יוסיף ה' שנית ידו״ and so on. And may the Holy One, blessed be He, in His mercy speedily hasten His deed — the deed of redemption, soon — and we will rejoice and be glad in Him." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> For what reason were they worthy that all these journeys be written in the Torah? Because they received Israel. And the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to give them reward, as it says (Isaiah 35:1–2): ״ישושום מדבר וציה ותגל ערבה ותפרח כחבצלת / פרוח תפרח ותגל אף גילת ורנן כבוד הלבנון נתן לה הדר הכרמל והשרון המה יראו כבוד ה' הדר אלהינו״. And if the wilderness, merely by receiving Israel, attains such — how much more so one who receives Torah scholars into his house! You find that the wilderness is destined to become settled, and the settled land to become wilderness: as it says (Malachi 1:3): ״ואת עשו שנאתי ואשים את הריו שממה״; the wilderness shall be settled, as it says (Isaiah 41:18): ״אשים מדבר לאגם מים״. You find that now there are no trees in the wilderness, but in the future there will be trees there, as it says (Isaiah 41:19): ״אתן במדבר ארז שטה והדס ועץ שמן״ and so on. Now there is no road in the wilderness, for it is all sand, and a caravan does not travel there except by night by the light of the stars; but in the future there will be a road there, as it says (Isaiah 43:19): ״אשים במדבר דרך״ and so on, and it is written (Isaiah 35:8): ״והיה שם מסלול ודרך״. Thus far in the Midrash Tanchuma."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:37:54.349691714Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}